Managing and Debugging a Production Environment
==============================================
Use top, htop, vmstat, or sar to monitor system performance (CPU, memory, disk I/O).

Check dmesg for hardware errors or kernel messages.

Investigate system logs (e.g., /var/log/syslog, /var/log/messages).

Check for resource limits using ulimit.

Look at network performance using tools like netstat or ss.

Run strace on the application to see system calls and resource usage.

Review server load balancing and scaling strategies (e.g., horizontal vs. vertical scaling).

If necessary, check the database performance (e.g., query bottlenecks).

Handling a Full Disk Scenario
==============================================================================
First, identify large files or directories using du -sh * or ncdu.

Investigate log files (/var/log) that may be consuming space and consider rotating them or archiving old logs.

Use df to check disk usage and lsof to find processes holding onto files.

Implement log rotation with logrotate to prevent future log buildup.

If needed, clean up old packages using apt-get autoremove or yum clean.

Consider adding more disk space or migrating data to external storage solutions.

System Performance Tuning
================================

Start with basic system metrics using top, htop, vmstat, iostat, and free.

Analyze web server logs for issues using apachectl or nginx -t.

Optimize TCP settings with sysctl for better network performance.

Use nginx or apache tuning parameters like worker_processes or KeepAliveTimeout.

Review the application’s code or database queries for optimization.

Use caching mechanisms (e.g., Varnish, Redis) to reduce load.

Consider setting up CDNs for static content delivery.

Server Resource Exhaustion
===================================================
Check memory usage with free -m and identify processes with top or htop.

Use ps aux --sort=-%mem to find the most memory-consuming processes.

If a specific process is leaking memory, restart it or increase its resource allocation.

Consider adding swap space temporarily or optimize the application to consume less memory.

Use ulimit to adjust memory limits or adjust service configurations to prevent memory overuse.

Slow Application Performance
===========================================

Start by reviewing the web server logs for errors.

Use top or htop to check for high CPU usage or resource contention.

Review database performance by checking slow queries in MySQL or PostgreSQL logs.

Use strace on the application to see system calls.

Check network latency using ping or traceroute.


Service Not Starting
===========================

Check service status with systemctl status <service>.

Review service-specific logs with journalctl -xe or /var/log/<service>.log.

Look for common issues like missing dependencies or file permissions.

Use strace or lsof to investigate any system calls or open files the service might be stuck on.

Check disk space with df or memory usage with free.

Disk Full on Production Server
=============================

Run df -h to identify which partition is full.

Use du -sh /path/to/dir/* to find large directories or files.

Clean up old logs or temporary files in /var/log and /tmp.

Use lsof to find processes holding onto deleted files and clear them.


